cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Japan Construction Cost Database: An Open Dataset for LLM-Based Cost Estimation and Fraud Detection in Residential Renovation"
abstract: >-
  An open, machine-readable construction-cost dataset for the Japanese
  market. JCCDB v3.1 provides an item catalogue of 95,403 items across 72 coarse
  categories (402 in the fine-grained schema), recording item names,
  categories and units only, with no embedded prices. It is released under
  a single CC BY 4.0 licence, in force since v2.1 (2026-07-11). The
  canonical file is jccdb-v3-full.csv. Two quality tiers are documented as
  data facts: a verified tier of 13,207 items checked against manufacturer
  catalogues, and a matrix-generated extended tier of 52,313 items whose
  individual SKU existence is not verified; 13,207 + 52,313 equals the
  total exactly, with zero duplicate rows. Of the verified items, roughly
  11,250 came from the v2.0 hand-check and carry no per-item evidence
  URLs; the remainder were promoted in the v3.0 and v3.1 rounds and carry
  per-item evidence URLs in jccdb-v3-provenance.csv. A further 608 items
  proven not to exist are held separately in jccdb-v3-retracted.csv with
  reasons and evidence URLs. Per-file SHA-256 digests are published in
  JCCDB_v3_1_RELEASE_DECLARATION.md and anchored to Bitcoin through the
  JIDEC ledger. Earlier copies of the extension obtained before 2026-07-11
  under CC BY-NC 4.0 may continue to be used under those terms. It extends JCCDB v1.1, which comprises
  87 construction plans across 7 renovation categories, annotated with
  88 fraud-detection patterns categorized by severity. Pricing in the
  v1.1 layer is derived from the HORIZON SHIELD (HS) Rule, a transparent
  cost formula grounded in 30 years of hands-on construction management
  practice combined with 2026 Kanto-region trade-price data, with each
  plan stratified across four contractor-size tiers and margin ranges
  drawn from Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism
  (MLIT) industry analysis. Released to support NLP/LLM research on
  Japanese construction cost estimation, consumer-protection studies,
  and cross-country comparative analysis. Methodology declaration
  time-stamped to Bitcoin block #949356 via OpenTimestamps. The dataset
  powers the HORIZON SHIELD verification service over MCP and A2A and
  the HORIZON SHIELD plugin in the ChatGPT plugin directory.
